        <body>
            <div xml:id="d547">

                <pb facs="0725" n="439" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg439"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/2921</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Laughlin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-09-04">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 4, 1916</hi>
                        </date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received 5.30 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4823. Your 3736, August 25. Foreign Office states that in view of assurances
                    given respecting genuine purchase of cocoa by American firm, a telegram has been
                    sent to British Consul, Guayaquil, instructing him that the shipment in question
                    should be facilitated and adds that the charterers of the ship have also been
                    notified no objection now exists to acceptance for shipment of this cargo.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Laughlin</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
